Hey Dan, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

10
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:14,720
I'm looking forward to it.

11
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:20,640
So today we're going to talk about app experimentation.

12
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,920
And I talked to a lot of different people about who I should have on the podcast.

13
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:29,600
And your name popped up, RevenueCat colleague.

14
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:31,920
You and I didn't get to talk enough internally.

15
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:34,800
So I had to bring you on the podcast to get to talk to you.

16
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:49,920
But you were at Tel Tech, which got acquired by IAC, where you collaborated with a lot of top apps in the App Store and just have so much experience in experimentation that I just thought it would be so fun to talk about that today.

17
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:55,440
And then, of course, we have a little surprise at the end that RevenueCat's launching a pretty cool new product.

18
00:01:56,080 --> 00:02:11,440
So to kick things off, I just wanted to get a kind of an overview of your thinking about experimentation, having done really sophisticated AB tests, having run the gamut of how to do in app experimentation.

19
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:13,200
So let's just kick it off there.

20
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:15,840
Like, what is experimentation?

21
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:16,640
Why do it?

22
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:17,680
How do you think about it?

23
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:19,040
Yeah, absolutely.

24
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:20,000
It's a great question.

25
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:22,720
It sounds like there should be like this really, really simple answer.

26
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:23,920
And I don't know that there is.

27
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:31,920
But I mean, really, when you're building products, when you're building anything, there are thousands of little micro decisions that you're making all the time.

28
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:36,000
And a lot of them are little experiments and little tests, right?

29
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:42,800
Like whether you design them in a really scientific way or not, like you're frequently testing a hypothesis that you have, right?

30
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,280
Like I think this product is going to be valuable to customers.

31
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:47,440
So I'm going to go build it and deploy it in the market.

32
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:50,800
You know, I think that this acquisition channel is going to work for me.

33
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:54,080
So I'm going to go spend some money on it and see if I can acquire customers that way.

34
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:56,720
It's true for every decision that you're making.

35
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:58,720
Experimentation in particular.

36
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:17,840
I think like AB testing in particular is basically a way to take that, that general idea, right, that like everything is a bet, everything is a test, and figure out, all right, for a very narrow decision, a very narrow test, how can we design that in such a way that it's easy to measure the impact of that on the other side of it, right?

37
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:26,800
How can I understand what the impact is of this change that I make, both the direct things that I expect to impact and maybe sometimes the indirect things that I don't expect to impact.

38
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:27,600
Right.

39
00:03:28,800 --> 00:04:11,840
So, and one of the interesting things about AB testing specifically is that it inherently is easy to revert.vert, but how do you think about that kind of spectrum between an AB test where it's like you flip it on, you flip it off, all the way to, you know, like a major version update that, you know, revamp the data format in the back end where you, you know, reverting, I mean, you're experimenting, you're trying something new, you're doing something different, but if it causes something that you didn't see, if it changes the user experience in a way you didn't understand, if it breaks, you know, reverting it can be incredibly difficult.

40
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:23,120
So how do you how do you think about that spectrum when kind of approaching decision making in, you know, building new features and, and experimenting with an app?

41
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:24,480
Yeah, absolutely.

42
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:28,320
I I mean, I think what you're getting at there is like risk mitigation basically, right?

43
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:36,480
Like how do we how do we make sure that like the bet that we're making, the experiment that we're running is sufficiently protected from the downside risks?

44
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:41,360
And there are some changes that you make that like, don't have really significant downside risks, right?

45
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:55,040
Like, um, maybe there's a particular benefit or feature that you want to add to your product that lots and lots of customers have asked for that it's really easy to for you to build, that you know for sure you want to build, and you're just going to say, all right, like, feel very confident in this bet.

46
00:04:55,280 --> 00:04:56,080
I'm going to make it.

47
00:04:56,240 --> 00:04:57,200
I'm going to ship it.

48
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:58,720
If no one uses it, that's okay.

49
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:04,320
If it, you know, the worst way it possibly breaks doesn't change my life, doesn't change my customer's life.

50
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:05,360
Awesome, right?

51
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:12,720
Like, that's probably a situation where you don't need to think like super, super hard about exactly how you want to experiment with it or or measure the impact of that.

52
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:16,000
Like, you've mitigated the risks just by the nature of what you're doing.

53
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:24,320
And then there are other types of changes, you know, like how you're going to monetize your product, the price you're going to offer it at, the durations you're going to offer it at, things like that.

54
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:27,520
that where the risk can be quite high, right?

55
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:37,840
Like, um, if you overpriced your product, like whatever that price elasticity curve is, if you pass the reasonable point of that curve and no one's interested, that's really bad for your business, right?

56
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:42,400
Like, and like, you don't know where that spot is before you test it, right?

57
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:52,000
Like, you know, most apps are unique enough that there's not necessarily someone else that you can point at and say, ah, the price is eight bucks a month and that's just like the right price in this market.

58
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:53,920
Like, no one has that information.

59
00:05:54,400 --> 00:06:05,920
And that's a case where then, like, AB testing, especially when it's remotely controlled and you can easily turn it on and off from the back end without waiting for the app review process or anything like that becomes really powerful, right?

60
00:06:06,080 --> 00:06:13,680
Because then you can still answer these really important questions for your business, but not do so in a way where you're incurring a ton of risk by doing that at the same time.

61
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:27,360
So as a product manager, when looking at this spectrum, is there a a kind of framework to think about like this is easy to change, easy to implement.

62
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:36,800
So kind of low cost from an engineering standpoint and or just, you know, time of the team's standpoint and then also, you know, low downside risk.

63
00:06:37,680 --> 00:06:41,360
So like, just ship it, let's move quick, let's break things, let's whatever.

64
00:06:41,840 --> 00:07:06,960
And then when you go down that, you know, deeper spectrum, is there, is there, you know, the further you get on the, you know, untested and hard to revert scale, is that where, you know, being more diligent about, you know, brainstorming downsides, you know, really talking to users beforehand, you know, maybe even prototyping and presenting it to users beforehand.

65
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:16,560
Is that how you kind of think about the spectrum is, is like just doing so much more diligence before implementing something that's hard to test and or hard to revert?

66
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:19,040
Yeah, yeah, it's a great question.

67
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:23,760
I'd frame it a little bit differently, but I think it's exactly the variables that you're getting at.

68
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:36,320
I think maybe the first set of questions I would be asking is for this change that I want to make, right, this bet that I want to make, is it testable or not, just kind of as a binary question.

69
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:40,640
And that really is a cost question more than anything else, right?

70
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:46,000
Like if you decided, no matter what, I'm going to test this change, there's probably a way to do it.

71
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:47,360
Software is very flexible, right?

72
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:52,400
We're very creative people like you can do it, but it's really a cost question at that point, right?

73
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:59,120
Like, is it worth spending weeks or months designing the perfect test for this particular problem that I have?

74
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:04,960
And is that particular problem, uh, is the downside risk high enough that it's worth testing?

75
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:08,480
Um, so that's kind of like the first question that you're asking.

76
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:13,840
But then I think on the other side of that, there's also the benefit that you get from testing something, right?

77
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:18,880
So like, first it's just the feasibility of whether this is possible, um, you know, in a reasonable time horizon.

78
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:21,600
But then it's the benefit that you get from testing as well, right?

79
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:30,640
So like a well designed AB test is going to tell you something about the impact of the variable that you're testing that you might not be able to discover otherwise.

80
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:34,000
And so it may be that it's a low risk variable.

81
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:55,040
It may be that the downside risk is not super concerning to you, but that the positive side of learning something about how much this feature matters to your customers, what the impact is of this particular change, what it tells you about their perception of your product, that the increased learning on the other side of that is worth it at a sufficiently low investment point.

82
00:08:55,520 --> 00:08:59,680
You're not going to spend months to answer some of those questions, but will you spend a couple of days, a couple of weeks?

83
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:03,200
Maybe, like, if it matters enough for your business, yeah, that's a great trade.

84
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:07,440
Yeah, that's that's a great way to kind of reframe what I was getting at there.

85
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:10,960
You know, it's just such a tough balancing act.

86
00:09:11,120 --> 00:09:13,520
I mean, there's so many factors in all of this.

87
00:09:14,960 --> 00:09:27,760
And one of the last factors I wanted to kind of hit on before we move on to other parts of the testing is where does product intuition sit in in this spectrum?

88
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,240
All the tension pulling different directions.

89
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:36,960
And you haven't had a chance to listen to this yet, but we actually, Jacob and I recorded at PyCon.

90
00:09:36,960 --> 00:09:38,240
a recorder at a podcast with Duolingo recently.

91
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:38,720
Yeah.

92
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:46,640
And they've kind of built a whole company around testing so that when an idea comes up, it's very low cost to implement.

93
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:52,320
They have systems to mitigate risk by rolling it out to, to small portions of the customers.

94
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:57,280
And their whole culture is around testing and they run so many tests.

95
00:09:57,360 --> 00:10:03,600
They know how to do tests that they can just throw stuff against the wall and, and see if it sticks.

96
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:07,280
But other companies don't.

97
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:15,760
And other companies are kind of, you know, Apple being a famous example, like they don't do it a ton of AB testing on and it's hard to do.

98
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:19,840
I mean, how do you AB test an API that you're releasing to developers?

99
00:10:20,160 --> 00:10:22,400
You know, you can't really AB test hardware.

100
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:25,360
I mean, there's there, there are limits to testability.

101
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:35,360
So, so that's just another really interesting spectrum from kind of like product autour where you just make the decisions and don't really necessarily care.

102
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:39,440
It's like, this is the product you want to build and you're just going to build it.

103
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:57,920
And then, you know, all the way to Duolingo, probably being one of the more extreme examples that we see in the industry of the whole company being built around and even kind of fashioning a moat of being able to experiment rapidly and improve the product in that way.

104
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:05,680
So, so where, yeah, how do you think about product intuition and the spectrum all the way to just test everything?

105
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:06,960
Yeah, yeah.

106
00:11:07,760 --> 00:11:16,640
So I've never spoken to anyone at Duolingo, but let me let me do my best to try to give the answer that I think they would give that probably puts it all together.

107
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:23,680
Duolingo has some kind of a product strategy and intuition that's right.

108
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:26,080
That's driving their in initial decisions, right?

109
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:30,800
Like they're in the teaching space at the broadest level, right?

110
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:33,760
I think maybe cut this out if I'm wrong.

111
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:35,200
I think they're building a math product.

112
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:36,560
I think I've seen marketing around that.

113
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:37,440
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

114
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:38,000
That's great.

115
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:38,960
I actually downloaded it.

116
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:39,680
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

117
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:40,640
I guess the marketing is working.

118
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:42,400
That's a real thing that I conjured up.

119
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:54,080
So like they have some sense of a product strategy already that they're that limits the possible directions that they're going to run in and the possible things that they're going to build.

120
00:11:55,120 --> 00:12:05,680
And now within that, there are a thousand micro decisions about how it's going to work and who it's going to be for and everything like that that probably are quite testable.

121
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:10,880
If you've done the upfront investment to like build the system that makes it really inexpensive to test those things.

122
00:12:12,320 --> 00:12:22,160
And that's really like, I think, a best of both world scenario where it's like, all right, we have a strategy that we're running down, a direction, and we've got lots of these little paths that we don't know which one to take.

123
00:12:22,400 --> 00:12:25,280
And we're probably not going to spend weeks getting hung up on which path to take either.

124
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:31,520
But we've made it so inexpensive for us to try to find out just like on a probable spectrum, which one is better for us.

125
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:35,040
And we'll use that probability to weigh which path we're going to go down.

126
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:36,720
Like, I think that that's really like the.

127
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:38,400
best case scenario, right?

128
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:46,560
It's not going to, if you're figuring out whether to be a company that designs products for teaching or not, AB testing is not going to help you, right?

129
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:50,160
That's just like devoid of a product strategy at that point.

130
00:12:50,640 --> 00:13:01,920
Or like a thesis on what you can build that's useful for customers in the world, but I definitely think that most of the micro decisions at the furthest end of that spectrum of how you're going to deliver it are theoretically testable.

131
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:04,720
And then it's just the cost benefit analysis of whether it's worth it.

132
00:13:05,440 --> 00:13:05,520
Yeah.

133
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:12,000
So product intuition as a higher level framework to push those decisions.

134
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:16,560
And then I think some of this comes down to company stage two.

135
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:37,920
And this is what I wanted to talk about next, is that early on the cost to test and be sophisticated about testing, the cost of just throwing things against the wall is probably a little bit higher where maybe a great team does have a little more product intuition where they're not throwing everything against the wall.

136
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:43,200
They're placing bets that are have a higher likelihood of a good outcome.

137
00:13:43,840 --> 00:13:47,920
So yeah, let's talk through that kind of how you think about testing.

138
00:13:49,200 --> 00:14:03,680
an early stage app and maybe even in in a kind of less sophisticated ways big bets to um to kind of moving through all the way to a dualingo where you have you know a more sophisticated stack built out to do as much testing as you want.

139
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:04,800
Yeah, totally.

140
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:23,600
And I mean, I think this is where like the framework of thinking about everything as a test is really helpful because then you don't necessarily have to design the perfect AB test to still think about something as an experiment that should have some kind of measurement on the other side of that, you know, to the to the best of your ability.

141
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:38,720
Because like the cost benefit that we've been talking about, like if you're a one person team and your product is, you know, less than a year old and you've got a mountain of things that you know you need to add to it, like designing the perfect AB test is not going to be high on your priority list and it shouldn't be right.

142
00:14:38,880 --> 00:14:39,520
Like the right.

143
00:14:39,920 --> 00:14:47,840
The upside you're going to get from that probably is not more significant than spending a couple of weeks delivering the benefit that your customers have been begging you for.

144
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:53,120
So yeah, I mean, you have to be doing the cost benefit analysis at every stage.

145
00:14:53,360 --> 00:15:02,480
And I think in the case where you can't design the perfect AB test, there are lots and lots of other ways to measure the result of the changes that you're making.

146
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:02,720
Right?

147
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:11,280
If you just ship it to the app store and look at whatever analytics tool you're using and filter that down to only folks on the new app version, like you're going to learn something.

148
00:15:11,440 --> 00:15:11,600
Right?

149
00:15:11,680 --> 00:15:12,480
It's not going to be perfect.

150
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:17,760
Things change between, you know, the week that you release the last version and the week that you release the new version.

151
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:22,800
Um, but for things that are sufficiently impactful, you can usually detect that as well.

152
00:15:22,960 --> 00:15:23,120
Right?

153
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:30,240
Like everyone in the app development world has done the opposite where they ship like a terrible bug and like quickly figure out that it was because of this app version.

154
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:30,560
Right?

155
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:34,480
So if you could do the invers, you could, you'd probably do the positive version of that too.

156
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:35,280
Right.

157
00:15:35,680 --> 00:15:38,560
It's harder with smaller changes and harder as you scale, right?

158
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:47,200
Like, nothing of this is perfect, but there's, there's totally still ways to run educated tests, even if they're not sophisticated tests at low cost.

159
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:48,160
Yeah.

160
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:51,360
And I think that's where some folks get hung up though.

161
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:58,480
And I mean, you know, a completely silly example would be, you know, AB testing two different colors on the buy button of a paywall.

162
00:15:58,560 --> 00:16:08,400
It's like, yeah, when you're early, there's probably much lower hanging fruit than, like, designing a test for the color of the, of the call to action button on your paywall.

163
00:16:08,720 --> 00:16:09,040
Totally.

164
00:16:09,280 --> 00:16:12,000
And I mean, even that's perfect too, because like, you could try it.

165
00:16:12,160 --> 00:16:14,960
You could change the color, you know, really sit in the new app version.

166
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:18,960
If you can't tell which one won, then you learn that it doesn't matter, right?

167
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:22,880
Like you don't you didn't learn which one won, but you did learn that it doesn't matter for you right now.

168
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:35,680
Another aspect of this kind of growing company theme is that the source of traffic, the type of users you attract, the customer base changes over time.

169
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:43,760
So I'm curious to hear your thoughts on, you know, at Tel Tech, I'm sure you all experimented with all different kinds of marketing channels.

170
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:54,400
And as the app grew, you had new, you know, totally new cust kind of customer base is coming in and maybe new features that attracted new users for a whole new job to be done.

171
00:16:55,280 --> 00:17:15,200
How do you think about both, you know, evolving the experimentation over time, but also kind of understanding that some of your past experience experiments might not be valid given these new cohorts coming in and then how those cohorts actually shape the results of the test even?

172
00:17:15,440 --> 00:17:15,520
Yeah.

173
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:16,080
Yeah.

174
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:17,600
It's a great question.

175
00:17:17,760 --> 00:17:36,640
It's a, I'll be honest and say it's a really hard one to perfectly account for when building products and designing tests, you know, like, based on my prior experience at Teltek, like, I can think of so many more instances where a conclusion that we made from a test became stale faster than we realized than the opposite, right?

176
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:42,080
Where like, you realize that your customer demographic has changed and now it's time to retest that hypothesis.

177
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:56,400
Um, so yeah, I mean, I guess like the one, uh, probably takeaway from that that I, I try to take to my, my experiences now is that like when you run a test, you are learning something about those customers at that time and your product in that situation.

178
00:17:56,960 --> 00:18:01,040
And it's probably not going to go stale tomale at some point, right?

179
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:08,160
Like you should have a pretty healthy skepticism about, uh, you know, whether that's going to be true forever or whether that's just true for your product right now.

180
00:18:08,720 --> 00:18:20,320
And then I guess maybe the, the correlated take away from that is for variables in your product that are sufficiently impactful, you should be thinking about what some kind of a regular testing cadence looks like, right?

181
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:25,600
Like where the paywall is, what's on it, how you're describing the benefits to your customers, right?

182
00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:30,080
Like those should be part of your recurring testing process, not like we did that in 2018.

183
00:18:30,320 --> 00:18:32,000
So like that's what our paywall looks like now.

184
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:32,880
Yeah.

185
00:18:33,600 --> 00:18:36,720
Uh, another correlated insight there that I have.

186
00:18:36,720 --> 00:18:49,760
I think there's one insight there that I just hadn't even thought of in quite this way before is that the results of tests in other apps are not necessarily and probably not directly applicable to your own app.

187
00:18:50,080 --> 00:19:05,440
So, you know, when another app has a ton of success putting the paywall before onboarding, you don't want to just automatically assume that's a best practice and that you should do it because someone else said that that worked for their app.

188
00:19:06,160 --> 00:19:10,720
How do you think about kind of filtering ideas from the ether and stuff we share on this podcast?

189
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:11,920
That stuff we'll talk about today.

190
00:19:12,640 --> 00:19:19,840
How do you think about that in terms of applying it across this entire industry with so many different apps, so many different experiences?

191
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:20,800
Yeah, totally.

192
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:21,920
I mean, look, it's tough.

193
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:23,120
It's judgment calls, right?

194
00:19:23,440 --> 00:19:28,320
Like you had JC Day on a couple of episodes ago with the Blinkist paywall.

195
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:34,240
I viscerally remember the first time I it was someone else on my team that showed me the Blinkist paywall.

196
00:19:34,320 --> 00:19:35,680
And I was like, that's an interesting idea.

197
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,720
And then you start seeing other people trying it and it works.

198
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:47,600
And that like quickly snowballs into like, oh, there's a there's like a potentially applicable piece of like truth here about what's an effective way to sell your product that we should try.

199
00:19:47,680 --> 00:19:49,200
And sure enough, we tried it it.

200
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:52,160
You know, it didn't have the same result in every one of our apps.

201
00:19:52,480 --> 00:19:55,840
We had a portfolio, so we had the luxury of testing it in different places.

202
00:19:56,080 --> 00:19:57,760
But yeah, like we saw some benefits too, right?

203
00:19:57,920 --> 00:20:06,080
Like that was a case where you see the change enough times and you see the impact enough times that it builds the confidence level that there's something real here now.

204
00:20:06,320 --> 00:20:15,120
At the same time, too, though, like there's going to be lots of other situations where you see some idea or have your own idea that doesn't necessarily have a lot of substantiation to it.

205
00:20:15,280 --> 00:20:15,440
Right?

206
00:20:15,520 --> 00:20:18,640
It's reasonable to suspect that it might help your business, right?

207
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:24,480
Like I should make it really obvious that there's a free trial on this product so that more customers, uh, join.

208
00:20:24,880 --> 00:20:26,960
and see if there's actual real benefit to it.

209
00:20:27,280 --> 00:20:29,840
Um, and you don't know if that's going to work, right?

210
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:43,760
And like, that's I think that's where it becomes so valuable to minimize the cost of running tests as much as possible because it you build up the incentives in your own decision making to say, yes, let's try it instead of, no, it's too expensive.

211
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:44,880
Yeah.

212
00:20:46,320 --> 00:21:04,080
And, and, and, and that's an interesting thought too because there's so many different ways to reduce the cost and the cost both in risk and, you know, one of the things you talked about earlier is how, you know, at scale, you can roll out some of these changes in an AB test to a smaller portion of the users.

213
00:21:04,240 --> 00:21:13,280
So you're mitigating the downside risk of it being a bad experiment, especially on something like a paywall where you're impacting monetization.

214
00:21:14,080 --> 00:21:28,000
But then you go all the way down to, you know, like tooling, you know, and as we were talking about for an early stage app, part of the way you minimize risk and reduce cost to do the experiment is just don't worry about being incredibly sophisticated.

215
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:30,560
You're probably not going to get statistical significance anyway.

216
00:21:30,800 --> 00:21:31,760
And we'll talk about that later.

217
00:21:32,560 --> 00:21:36,400
But do it in the, the cheap way and design the test in a way.

218
00:21:37,120 --> 00:21:43,120
that you can see the result without spending all that extra time, effort, tooling and all those other things.

219
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:44,160
Yeah, yeah.

220
00:21:44,640 --> 00:21:48,480
And even like that, the Blinkist paywall example is such a good version of this.

221
00:21:48,880 --> 00:21:51,520
For anyone that's not familiar, it's basically two parts, right?

222
00:21:51,760 --> 00:22:03,600
One is on the paywall show the customer how their trial is going to work and then additionally send them a push notification at some point during that process so that they know the trial is about to end and they have this prompt to take the action.

223
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:09,360
If it's too expensive to add the push notification, like take the learning of like explain the trial, right?

224
00:22:09,680 --> 00:22:12,480
Like make it really clear to them on the paywall how the trial is going to work.

225
00:22:13,120 --> 00:22:17,200
That's, I'm certain, less time and energy than building the push notification.

226
00:22:17,360 --> 00:22:23,200
Like you could, you could always slim down, uh, ideas like that to something that is a reasonable cost for you to take on.

227
00:22:23,840 --> 00:22:40,000
And then, and then push that in an app update and look at the, uh, uh, paywall to free trial conversion rate the week before and then the trial conversion rate to, to paid and then take those same three metrics and look at them the next week.

228
00:22:40,560 --> 00:22:42,720
And if you see a huge difference, it was a success.

229
00:22:43,200 --> 00:22:47,840
If it's about the same, that probably didn't hurt.

230
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:49,600
If it goes down, reverse it.

231
00:22:49,840 --> 00:22:54,480
And it's probably not that hard to just go ahead and reverse the paywall back to what you had before.

232
00:22:54,720 --> 00:22:55,760
So totally, totally.

233
00:22:55,920 --> 00:22:58,960
And you can go design the perfect AB test at that point too, right?

234
00:22:59,120 --> 00:23:01,520
It's like, okay, we've got enough signal that this might matter.

235
00:23:01,840 --> 00:23:03,120
Let's go do the perfect analysis.

236
00:23:03,600 --> 00:23:04,320
You can always do that.

237
00:23:04,960 --> 00:23:05,120
Yeah.

238
00:23:06,480 --> 00:23:10,320
So we've kind of been talking theory and generalities.

239
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:15,200
I did want to take this into a more practical level.

240
00:23:15,680 --> 00:23:17,520
So kind of a testing one hundred and one.

241
00:23:17,840 --> 00:23:22,960
And I mean, to be honest, you know, I have never run very sophisticated tests in my own apps.

242
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:23,520
I'm indie.

243
00:23:23,920 --> 00:23:28,480
I'm very much biased toward the, oh, just ship it and see what happens.

244
00:23:28,880 --> 00:23:28,960
Yeah.

245
00:23:29,600 --> 00:23:31,280
So I'll kind of share my bias.

246
00:23:32,560 --> 00:23:40,480
But because of that, you know, I personally don't have a super deep understanding of how to run tests well.

247
00:23:41,280 --> 00:23:44,480
And then I think our audience probably lands on a spectrum.

248
00:23:44,720 --> 00:23:54,480
And even if you have done much more sophisticated testing, kind of revisiting the fundamentals with someone who's done it before, I think will be really valuable.

249
00:23:54,800 --> 00:24:05,600
So that's what I wanted to do next, is just kind of talk through the steps of a good kind of testing plan and how to actually implement these kind of ideas that we've been talking about.

250
00:24:06,400 --> 00:24:12,080
So the first step is to form a hypothesis, which I think a lot of people skip, which is funny.

251
00:24:13,040 --> 00:24:14,240
And I mean, I've done this.

252
00:24:14,400 --> 00:24:16,640
It's like, okay, I'm just going to add this feature.

253
00:24:17,120 --> 00:24:20,560
And if I build it, they will come, it's going to make all this difference.

254
00:24:20,880 --> 00:24:27,440
But I don't really ever sit down and say, okay, by changing the paywall in this way, I think this is going to happen.

255
00:24:27,760 --> 00:24:29,120
And this is how I'm going to measure it.

256
00:24:29,280 --> 00:24:30,000
So yeah.

257
00:24:30,400 --> 00:24:34,720
How do you think about forming a hypothesis before doing anything else?

258
00:24:35,200 --> 00:24:35,840
Yeah, totally.

259
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:42,000
I think there's a there's a couple of subtle benefits to it that you learn by not doing it.

260
00:24:42,160 --> 00:24:43,040
Unfortunately, right?

261
00:24:43,200 --> 00:24:46,800
Like this is a trial by fire, uh, uh, type of learning.

262
00:24:47,120 --> 00:24:54,480
But, um, I mean, first, like when you're making a change to your product, whether you say it out loud or not, you have some kind of goal in mind, right?

263
00:24:54,640 --> 00:24:56,880
There's a thing that you're looking to achieve.

264
00:24:57,280 --> 00:24:57,920
It's fine.

265
00:24:58,080 --> 00:25:01,120
Sometimes if you don't write it down, it's really helpful if you do write it down.

266
00:25:01,280 --> 00:25:06,160
That way, you can, you have kind of this starting framework of like, okay, I think we're going to go do X.

267
00:25:06,560 --> 00:25:10,000
If we do X, that's going to yield this in our business, this for our customers, right?

268
00:25:10,240 --> 00:25:12,720
Like I've got my thesis on paper of why this outcome.

269
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:14,640
or this goal is worth striving for.

270
00:25:15,120 --> 00:25:19,600
And then beyond that, I've got my thesis for why this particular change is going to result in that.

271
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:26,400
Where that becomes really critical is when the test does something that you don't expect, which happens all the time, right?

272
00:25:26,720 --> 00:25:32,240
Like, and again, you could think of this in the non testing realm when you make any change to your product.

273
00:25:32,560 --> 00:25:35,840
Like, how many times do you discover something that you didn't expect from that?

274
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:37,040
Like, almost every time.

275
00:25:38,240 --> 00:25:40,800
And so that's certainly true with AB tests that you run.

276
00:25:41,360 --> 00:25:51,280
And then if you've written down the goal that you have initially, written down what you expect to happen from the test, you have the test., you have your results, you can see whether or not it does that.

277
00:25:51,600 --> 00:25:54,320
You're learning something about the variables in your product, right?

278
00:25:54,560 --> 00:26:02,240
You're learning something about the impact that they actually do have, how that does and doesn't match your expectations, and therefore how your expectations need to change in the future.

279
00:26:03,760 --> 00:26:14,160
The other part of this, too, that I would be remiss if I didn't hit on, is when you write down what you expect to happen in advance, that gives you the prompt to go, make sure that you can measure the thing that you're hoping to happen.

280
00:26:14,320 --> 00:26:14,480
Right.

281
00:26:14,800 --> 00:26:22,720
And it has happened, lots and lots of times for me, that I want to measure X, and I run the test, and I go to measure X, and I can't measure X.

282
00:26:22,960 --> 00:26:26,880
And like, there are a few more frustrating moments than that when it comes to experimentation.

283
00:26:27,280 --> 00:26:27,360
Yeah.

284
00:26:27,600 --> 00:26:31,760
Well, and that leads us to kind of the next step is then to actually design the test.

285
00:26:32,160 --> 00:26:32,240
Yeah.

286
00:26:32,480 --> 00:26:38,800
So you've you form the hypothesis and you make sure it is measurable.

287
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:40,960
Now, how do you design the test?

288
00:26:41,680 --> 00:26:41,760
Yeah.

289
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:49,120
I some of the kind of high level questions that I think you'd be asking at this point and deciding on, first is just like what tools are you going to use, right?

290
00:26:49,680 --> 00:26:51,280
That's a lot of what we've talked about so far.

291
00:26:51,440 --> 00:27:00,160
Like, is it a test where you're just going to make the change in your product and release it to everyone and try to measure between different time periods what the impact was?

292
00:27:00,160 --> 00:27:00,240
was.

293
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:08,320
Do you have some kind of AB testing solution where you can in the app split those changes in a perfect world?

294
00:27:08,880 --> 00:27:17,520
Do you have an AB testing solution where you can make those changes remotely and then you're not dependent on an app release to turn on and off the test that you're running?

295
00:27:17,840 --> 00:27:27,840
And then even beyond that, too, do you have the right filtering in place both for which customers you want to expose the test to and then how to measure the result on the other side of that, right?

296
00:27:28,080 --> 00:27:31,520
So like just as an example, you're let's do the blinkest paywall again.

297
00:27:31,680 --> 00:27:33,040
You're building your blinkest paywall.

298
00:27:33,040 --> 00:27:35,360
You're going and releasing it to your customers as an AB test.

299
00:27:35,920 --> 00:27:38,240
That's app version 6.0.

300
00:27:38,960 --> 00:27:47,040
You need to only measure customers that are in app version 6.0 because there's still going to be other people using your app that might hit your paywall that will never see that experience because it wasn't there.

301
00:27:47,280 --> 00:27:47,760
Right.

302
00:27:48,400 --> 00:27:56,720
And so you've got to make sure you know exactly which customers are going to get exposed to this test and make sure that you can measure just the impact on those customers on the other side of it.

303
00:27:57,040 --> 00:28:05,040
Yeah, I think those are kind of like the biggest questions I'd be asking when designing the test to make sure that the thing that I have in mind is actually achievable and measurable through the tools that I have.

304
00:28:05,600 --> 00:28:05,680
Yeah.

305
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:09,840
In designing a test, have you ever or heard of someone?

306
00:28:09,920 --> 00:28:11,360
Because I was just thinking about this.

307
00:28:12,960 --> 00:28:37,760
Specifically done user follow up like this user saw a or this cohort of users saw a and we're going to send them a prompt to do a survey and this these users saw b and we're going to send them a prompt to do the survey and look at actual like quantitative data from your user base versus just trying to like figure out the results from a qualitative standpoint.

308
00:28:38,080 --> 00:28:39,280
Yeah, yeah, it's a great question.

309
00:28:39,920 --> 00:28:53,280
I'm sorry to say I can't think of many like specific examples where I've done that personally but I can absolutely imagine where especially for tests that don't draw an obvious reason for their conclusion, right?

310
00:28:53,520 --> 00:28:59,840
Like we've been talking about paywall testing a lot and maybe, maybe paywall testing is, uh, is not the best example for this, right?

311
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:09,600
If you're changing your price point, that probably does change the customer's perception of the value of the product, but maybe in like really subconscious ways that would be hard to pull out in a conversation.

312
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,720
Whereas like, we'll use the blink as paywall again, right?

313
00:29:13,040 --> 00:29:24,160
I, uh, if I use that design to show you explicitly how the trial is going to work, I could totally see the benefit in talking to that customer on day three of their trial and trying to understand, do you view this product differently?

314
00:29:24,720 --> 00:29:25,920
Do you see the risks of it differently?

315
00:29:26,160 --> 00:29:32,240
Do you feel safer using it than you would have otherwise if you saw a paywall on day one and think you're kind of stuck here more or less?

316
00:29:33,440 --> 00:29:40,320
So no, I've not done that, but I think it's an excellent addition to the quantitative data that you get from the test itself.

317
00:29:40,960 --> 00:29:40,960
Yeah.

318
00:29:42,080 --> 00:29:43,520
Yeah, I think that would be really interesting.

319
00:29:44,480 --> 00:29:58,560
I don't know exactly how you would set that up, but yeah, it could be a really interesting kind of qualitative way to measure the results of a hypothesis, especially since you really can't get only so much from hard data.

320
00:29:58,960 --> 00:30:00,560
And we talk about it a lot in the podcast.

321
00:30:00,560 --> 00:30:06,880
I've talked to a lot in the podcast, I've talked to a lot of different apps where, you know, some of their bigger insights came from actually talking to users.

322
00:30:07,040 --> 00:30:10,960
And so incorporating that into a testing scheme could be really powerful.

323
00:30:11,440 --> 00:30:11,520
Yeah.

324
00:30:11,760 --> 00:30:14,160
And just a note to on how you might accomplish it.

325
00:30:15,040 --> 00:30:41,520
Little tip, I would definitely recommend when you run a test to your customers, an AB test in particular, if you're able to tag that fact to that customer's profile in some way, definitely do it like a user attribute or an event that tells you which variant they saw, whatever the case may be, six months from now, you might be seeing some change in your product and you're not sure what it's for, being able to see which customers are driving that change and whether they were exposed to something unusual in the product is hugely valuable.

326
00:30:42,160 --> 00:31:00,720
Yeah, that's such a great point is that, yeah, when you're designing the test, make sure you tag the users who saw it in a meaningful way that you can go back and understand over the long haul because you're going to measure that, we'll talk about it more later, but you're going to measure the results at one point in time, but those results can change over time.

327
00:31:01,200 --> 00:31:01,440
Totally.

328
00:31:02,400 --> 00:31:05,680
So step three, sanity check.

329
00:31:06,080 --> 00:31:12,400
Tell me about why and how to sanity check a design plan or a test.

330
00:31:13,520 --> 00:31:14,240
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

331
00:31:14,880 --> 00:31:25,040
It partially gets back to the idea that I was referring to before that it can be, it can be so easy to create a change, right?

332
00:31:25,280 --> 00:31:31,280
Create a test that you want to run and assume it's going to answer the question that you have and then go run it and find that that's not the case.

333
00:31:32,240 --> 00:31:38,320
And so this sounds kind of obvious, but it really is very important at this point to say, all right, I'm going to make this change.

334
00:31:38,720 --> 00:31:41,600
It's going to be exposed to this customer, you know, for this amount of time.

335
00:31:42,160 --> 00:31:44,480
As a result, I think this measurement is going to change.

336
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:48,720
Now I'm going to go try to find that measurement in the existing tools.

337
00:31:49,120 --> 00:31:55,200
that I have today and make sure that I can split my data accurately to understand what the impact of that was.

338
00:31:55,520 --> 00:31:59,920
And then even beyond that, too, it's not just whether I can measure the change that I'm making or not.

339
00:32:00,320 --> 00:32:06,480
It's also does this thing that I'm measuring actually prove or disprove my hypothesis, right?

340
00:32:07,520 --> 00:32:19,520
Does the goal that I set out to answer, the thing that I want to learn about my product or my customers, is it answered by this data point or is there something else that I need to be tracking and adding to this to actually have a full picture of that?

341
00:32:20,400 --> 00:32:32,640
I'm curious as a PM at Tel Tech, was there kind of amongst other PMs or when you eventually were head of product, was there kind of a process for this?

342
00:32:32,880 --> 00:32:34,720
Like, okay, I've designed my testing plan.

343
00:32:35,040 --> 00:32:42,960
Like, I want at least one other PM or engineer or someone else at the company to kind of sign off on it before we actually, you know, drop it into the app.

344
00:32:43,440 --> 00:32:43,520
Yeah.

345
00:32:43,840 --> 00:32:43,920
Yeah.

346
00:32:44,160 --> 00:32:45,840
So I can't take total credit for this.

347
00:32:46,240 --> 00:32:52,960
I had one of the PMs on my team read the checklist manifesto and started preaching the fact that like, we should probably have checklists for more things.

348
00:32:53,840 --> 00:32:56,160
We said, Yeah, we really should have checklists for more things.

349
00:32:56,880 --> 00:33:00,800
And so we ended up developing just a simple checklist every time you run an AB test.

350
00:33:00,800 --> 00:33:01,120
And then you're going to do an AB test.

351
00:33:01,760 --> 00:33:03,920
But yeah, it's exactly for that purpose.

352
00:33:04,160 --> 00:33:10,080
One is kind of like for your own personal sanity to say, all right, did I actually set this up the way I intended?

353
00:33:10,320 --> 00:33:11,600
Have I done everything I need to do?

354
00:33:11,840 --> 00:33:15,760
And then part of that was what we call the peer review where you just send that checklist to someone else.

355
00:33:16,080 --> 00:33:19,760
They go through it, they check everything and they check it off that they've done the peer review.

356
00:33:19,920 --> 00:33:20,000
Yeah.

357
00:33:20,640 --> 00:33:22,480
Most of the time you find nothing, right?

358
00:33:22,720 --> 00:33:23,680
Most of the time that's just great.

359
00:33:23,840 --> 00:33:26,160
It looks good, but it's the same idea as code reviews, right?

360
00:33:26,400 --> 00:33:30,640
Like if the change that you're making is sufficiently important, you should have someone take a look at it beforehand.

361
00:33:30,800 --> 00:33:34,320
And so checklists are the non-tech version of doing the same thing.

362
00:33:34,800 --> 00:33:34,880
Yeah.

363
00:33:36,240 --> 00:33:43,920
Part of sanity checking the plan is also, and this is where I wanted to get into statistical significance.

364
00:33:44,240 --> 00:33:49,440
So it's something I don't really, I'm not a mathematician.

365
00:33:50,400 --> 00:33:56,000
I did advance like physics and stuff, but not statistics in college, so this is an area I'm weak in.

366
00:33:56,080 --> 00:34:11,680
And I think a lot of people in the industry aren't mathematicians themselves either, but are trying to measure results and understand whether those results are meaningful or not.

367
00:34:11,920 --> 00:34:12,320
So yeah.

368
00:34:12,320 --> 00:34:16,160
So, when you're sanity checking, that's probably one of the things you do, right?

369
00:34:17,440 --> 00:34:26,640
Is this a something that we're going to measure in a sophisticated way that we're going to understand statistical significance or is it something we're going to measure in a less sophisticated way?

370
00:34:26,960 --> 00:34:27,040
Yeah.

371
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:41,280
Um, and so then, so first, and I've just wanted to do this for a while, just have someone like explain statistical significance, like I'm five or maybe like I'm post college and actually do have some math background.

372
00:34:41,440 --> 00:34:43,760
You're your product manager on the first day of the job.

373
00:34:44,560 --> 00:34:45,360
Yeah, there you go.

374
00:34:45,680 --> 00:34:56,480
And then, and then let's talk about then how you filter the design against the sanity of actually being able to get those results or having to measure it in a different way.

375
00:34:56,960 --> 00:34:57,680
Yeah, totally.

376
00:34:58,400 --> 00:35:10,000
So I guess the first major caveat is that if you put a piece of paper in front of me and said, you know, figure out how to measure statistical significance like without using like the calculators that exist online, I would fail that test, right?

377
00:35:10,240 --> 00:35:21,840
Like, but I think the important part is to just understand what it is saying so that you know what it is not saying and then can, uh, can filter your assumptions and your, uh, your understanding accordingly.

378
00:35:22,240 --> 00:35:35,040
Basically what statistical significance is measuring it's frequently expressed as 90% statistical significance or 95% or 99% that's the p value of the the analysis that you're doing.

379
00:35:35,840 --> 00:35:50,720
If something is, if a change that you've made on your variant is 99% statistically significant, basically what that's saying is that there's a 1% chance that the opposite is true and your control group in this case performs better than your variant group.

380
00:35:51,440 --> 00:35:51,840
Gotcha.

381
00:35:52,400 --> 00:35:56,320
Importantly, it says absolutely nothing about the degree of success, right?

382
00:35:56,640 --> 00:36:05,200
So like it's saying in 99% of circumstances, this variant is performs ever so slightly or maybe significantly better than the control does, right?

383
00:36:05,440 --> 00:36:08,560
It doesn't tell you that it's a ten percent or twenty or fifty percent winner.

384
00:36:09,040 --> 00:36:09,200
Right.

385
00:36:09,920 --> 00:36:32,560
And then with the actual math behind that is that the higher degree of the result, so if you showed a control and a new paywall and the new paywall performed forty percent better, then you actually do need fewer paywall completions to actually get to statistical significance, correct?

386
00:36:32,720 --> 00:36:35,600
Exactly, exactly, which intuitively makes sense once you understand that, right?

387
00:36:36,240 --> 00:36:50,240
Like if all I'm trying to do is measure the probability that the variant is any degree better than the control, then I could do that by running this test for a really, really long time and seeing a small difference, or I could do it for a short time and see a very significant difference, right?

388
00:36:50,560 --> 00:36:57,600
It doesn't tell me that that very significant difference is going to hold, but it tells me there's a really good chance this is better than the control that I've been operating under.

389
00:36:58,160 --> 00:37:01,280
So there's actually that other variable that you just mentioned, which is time.

390
00:37:02,160 --> 00:37:12,320
And so, you know, again, someone like Duolingo operating at scale with, you know, whatever it is, tens of millions of daily active users, you can expose a test.

391
00:37:12,560 --> 00:37:19,760
test to a very small percentage of those users for a very small amount of time and get statistically significant results.

392
00:37:20,560 --> 00:37:28,320
But for, let's say, a smaller app to get to statistical significance, you're going to have to show it for a much longer time period.

393
00:37:28,960 --> 00:37:32,880
How do you think about time influencing the results?

394
00:37:33,040 --> 00:37:48,880
Where if you have to run a test for a month to get to statistical significance, is it even worth trying to measure statistical significance because of other changes and other variables that may be impacting those results over such a large time scale?

395
00:37:49,360 --> 00:37:50,080
Yeah, totally.

396
00:37:50,240 --> 00:37:58,800
I mean, I think the scientists of the world will yell at me for this, but I think if you make statistical significance like the holy grail, right?

397
00:37:59,200 --> 00:38:09,600
The barometer that like we have to meet this particular threshold or we can't run the test, that can get really dangerous really quickly because again, if you just think about what it's measuring, right?

398
00:38:09,680 --> 00:38:13,840
There's a, let's say there's a 90% chance that this variant outperforms the control.

399
00:38:14,800 --> 00:38:16,240
What if there's a 70% chance?

400
00:38:16,400 --> 00:38:18,080
What if there's a 50% chance, right?

401
00:38:18,400 --> 00:38:30,720
Like there, there are plenty of other thresholds within that where you as someone with finite time, energy and resources and lots of other things that you'd rather spend your time doing are comfortable saying, yeah, I'm going to take the 70-30 bet that this might be better, right?

402
00:38:30,960 --> 00:38:34,640
Because I've, I've, again, mitigated that risk in all of these other ways, right?

403
00:38:34,880 --> 00:38:44,480
Maybe you've looked at the, the daily results of the experiment that you're running, and it shows that the variant is better than the control in 95% of days, right?

404
00:38:44,640 --> 00:38:46,640
That's not statistical significance.

405
00:38:46,800 --> 00:38:53,680
That's not the same thing as the other calculation, but that tells me something about the probability that this might be better than the control is, right?

406
00:38:53,920 --> 00:39:03,360
So there's, there's other ways that you can look at your data to get to similar confidence levels that do not tell you statistical significance, but they do tell you something about the risk.

407
00:39:03,600 --> 00:39:08,880
And like that, that's really what we're trying to mitigate here is the risk of us getting this decision wrong.

408
00:39:09,200 --> 00:39:11,200
Yeah, that's such a great way to frame it.

409
00:39:13,040 --> 00:39:16,640
Ultimately about risk mitigation.

410
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:23,760
Yeah, it's just making experiments and understanding the risk and the reward and then making decisions.

411
00:39:23,920 --> 00:39:36,800
And that was actually the next place I wanted to go as well is that in this sanity check and I love that you had checklists at Teltek, where does user experience impact and product intuition kind of fit in?

412
00:39:36,800 --> 00:39:37,440
intuition kind of fit into it.

413
00:39:37,600 --> 00:39:38,160
Like, yeah.

414
00:39:39,040 --> 00:39:42,720
At some point you probably design a test and then you just say, you know what?

415
00:39:43,040 --> 00:39:50,400
This is just not the direction I want the product to go even if this test would show a meaningful result.

416
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:53,120
Where does that fit in?

417
00:39:53,600 --> 00:39:54,320
Yeah, totally.

418
00:39:55,280 --> 00:39:55,920
It's tough.

419
00:39:56,080 --> 00:40:03,120
I mean, ideally you've been asking some of these questions throughout the whole cycle of designing the experiment, right?

420
00:40:03,280 --> 00:40:05,360
Like forming your hypothesis and designing the test.

421
00:40:06,240 --> 00:40:15,840
But yeah, I mean, before you actually run it, it's critical that you've at least got a view on like does this thing that I'veve designed align up with the value proposition of my product?

422
00:40:16,240 --> 00:40:22,000
Does it align up with the way I want my customers to feel and think about the product?

423
00:40:24,240 --> 00:40:32,160
If you imagine a different world where you're the only person working on this product, there's a really good chance you've done those things without consciously asking about it.

424
00:40:33,040 --> 00:40:36,080
You're not going to design something that is appalling to you.

425
00:40:37,120 --> 00:40:41,840
But as you have teams of people and larger and larger teams working on products, like everyone's got differences of opinion.

426
00:40:42,160 --> 00:40:48,320
So it is really important that you're taking a step back and saying, all right, does this theoretical test that we could run?

427
00:40:49,120 --> 00:40:52,320
Is this really the product we would want to build if we built it from scratch?

428
00:40:52,560 --> 00:40:55,200
Does this align with our values, our strategy, our mission?

429
00:40:55,520 --> 00:40:58,000
And look, like most of the time, the answer there is going to be yes.

430
00:40:58,160 --> 00:41:05,280
Like it's pretty rarely that you get that far down the path and it's like, wait a second, like this does not advance learning in our products, right?

431
00:41:05,440 --> 00:41:08,320
But like, but it's still critical, right?

432
00:41:08,480 --> 00:41:19,280
Because like if that's, if that's missing, then you end up with just like a hodgepodge of experiences and decisions that don't align up into a cohesive product for your customers because they don't see it as a bunch of micro decisions.

433
00:41:19,760 --> 00:41:22,480
They see it as one hopefully cohesive experience.

434
00:41:22,880 --> 00:41:23,040
Right.

435
00:41:23,360 --> 00:41:24,240
You sold them through your marketing.

436
00:41:24,880 --> 00:41:28,560
And so, like, you've got to make sure like your product lines up with that pitch that you made to them.

437
00:41:29,360 --> 00:41:30,000
Yeah, totally.

438
00:41:30,560 --> 00:41:39,120
It was interesting talking to Duolingo because there internally there is this sanity check with, I believe he said, the CEO.

439
00:41:39,280 --> 00:41:39,360
Yeah.

440
00:41:39,600 --> 00:41:53,120
Where it's like, you know, if they were making kind of big product changes, even if something won, the final kind of check box was, you know, does the CEO think this is where we want to take the product?

441
00:41:53,840 --> 00:41:53,920
Yeah.

442
00:41:54,240 --> 00:41:55,280
Yeah, it's really interesting.

443
00:41:56,000 --> 00:41:59,680
So the next step is to actually test, to run the test.

444
00:42:00,880 --> 00:42:09,360
So that's pretty obvious, like you've made the plan and it's time to deploy the plan, but there are a lot of potential pitfalls.

445
00:42:10,160 --> 00:42:22,720
So tell me about, you know, some of the, some of the ways you can screw up actually running the test that, that again, probably should be part of your checklist and part of the design, but that you just want to make sure as you're running the test that you check in on.

446
00:42:23,120 --> 00:42:23,680
Yeah, totally.

447
00:42:24,640 --> 00:42:39,760
So maybe the first big thing that I'd call out here is that when you, when you choose to run something as an AB test, right, where you're, uh, remotely or in some way differing the experience that your customers get, one of the costs associatedated with that is that you're making it somewhat harder to test as well.

448
00:42:39,920 --> 00:42:40,080
Right.

449
00:42:40,320 --> 00:42:46,160
Like, you know, if the, if every customer gets the same experience, then just download the app on test flight and see if it works.

450
00:42:46,320 --> 00:42:48,720
You know, like it's, it's not that, that complex.

451
00:42:49,120 --> 00:43:00,720
So when you're designing that, that AB test initially, and certainly before you, you know, press the start button to actually run the test, um, you've got to make sure you actually test those changes that you've made in the product and that they work as intended.

452
00:43:01,360 --> 00:43:02,800
And that can be time consuming, right?

453
00:43:02,960 --> 00:43:08,640
If you haven't thought about it beforehand, like thought about a way to, to set that up, uh, like that can catch you by surprise.

454
00:43:08,880 --> 00:43:12,560
And that, just like with running the test in the first place, the more friction.

455
00:43:13,120 --> 00:43:19,280
there is in testing that experience and making sure it's working properly, the easier it is to say, ah, don't worry about it.

456
00:43:19,360 --> 00:43:20,560
Let's just run it and see what happens.

457
00:43:20,720 --> 00:43:20,880
Right.

458
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:28,640
Like you're trying to incentivize yourself to do these prudent steps in between to make sure that everything is working properly.

459
00:43:28,960 --> 00:43:34,560
And then other than that, like once the test itself is actually running, you don't want to be a slave to your data, right?

460
00:43:34,800 --> 00:43:37,440
You don't want to be looking at it like every hour, every day.

461
00:43:37,680 --> 00:43:43,600
Like, you know, I I've certainly been the victim of like looking at something after one day and getting really excited.

462
00:43:43,920 --> 00:43:48,160
And then, you know, having to like shuffle my way out of the room later when it's like, ah, that didn't do anything.

463
00:43:48,240 --> 00:43:49,680
I just got excited the first day.

464
00:43:50,880 --> 00:43:53,760
But at the same time, like, you should be paying attention, right?

465
00:43:54,240 --> 00:44:04,320
If, uh, for example, if you're changing something about your paywall that you're expecting to drive more conversions and you see a couple of days of like plummeting conversions, that's probably something, right?

466
00:44:04,480 --> 00:44:05,280
Like, that's Yeah.

467
00:44:05,680 --> 00:44:07,520
Again, we'll go back to statistical significance.

468
00:44:07,680 --> 00:44:14,080
You haven't learned anything statistically significant, but your product intuition is going off that like, something wrong is happening here that we should investigate.

469
00:44:14,320 --> 00:44:16,640
Um, you definitely pay close attention to the data.

470
00:44:17,040 --> 00:44:21,280
Just be careful not to draw conclusions until you've, you've let it run for a sufficient amount of time.

471
00:44:22,320 --> 00:44:22,400
Yeah.

472
00:44:22,720 --> 00:44:24,880
And one of the things we probably should have talked about.

473
00:44:25,680 --> 00:44:34,160
in designing the plan, but I think also it's something to double check as you're running the tests is to make sure that you're actually splitting well.

474
00:44:34,560 --> 00:44:34,640
Yes.

475
00:44:35,520 --> 00:44:37,520
That can be a huge issue.

